  "last_executor_claim": "Report written to `/app/report.md`.\n\nSummary of what it covers:\n\n- **What Ostonia is doing:** retirement age 60→65 phased by birth cohort, final-salary → 20-year career-average formula over an actuarial divisor (140), a 4%/yr deferral bonus, and a 35-year-service exemption.\n- **The rationale:** pay-as-you-go systems facing a rising old-age dependency ratio can only be balanced via higher contributions, lower benefits, or later retirement — Ostonia pulls two of those three levers. Raising the retirement age shortens benefit duration and extends contribution years; career averaging removes the late-career earnings bump and compresses costs; the divisor structure mimics NDC designs with built-in sustainability.\n- **Expected consequences:** modest GDP/employment gains (~0.2 pp/yr in French models; +10–19 pp employment in Austria and Finland), but significant spillovers into unemployment and disability, regressive incidence on lower-income workers, higher wealth inequality, and substantial political risk.\n- **Key caveat:** Ostonia's green paper is a framework with no actuarial valuation, so whether the reform actually restores solvency — or whether divisor 140 and the 4% bonus are fair or stealthy cuts — cannot be determined from the materials provided.",
